  "query_preview": "..After a dry weekend, we will start to see some\nchanges in the pattern beginning today. An upper low just offshore\nthe California coast will move just north of Las Vegas by this\nafternoon and then northeast through Utah on Tuesday. By late this\nafternoon through the overnight hours, an increase in instability\nprimarily northwest of Flagstaff will result in a 30-50% chance \nof showers and thunderstorms. Moisture levels are not impressive\nwith this initial round of showers, so generally light rainfall\namounts are expected. By Tuesday, precipitable water values\nincrease significantly across eastern Arizona bringing a 40-60%\nchance of showers/thunderstorms. Isolated storms will be possible\nfarther west. By Tuesday night through Wednesday - a plume of deep\nmoisture is forecast to move northward into New Mexico, with\neastern Arizona on the fringes of the plume. A disturbance moving\nnorthward out of Mexico should enhance precipitation chances\nespecially in eastern Arizona - with a marginal risk of flash \nflooding included in the latest WPC outlooks now including areas\nas far west as Prescott/Flagstaff, though the heaviest rainfall is  \nexpected to be east of the AZ/NM state line. Thursday - weak \ntroughing off the southern CA and Baja coasts is forecast to shift\nnortheastward, pulling some deeper moisture farther west to \ninclude most of northern AZ. There is still uncertainty in this \nscenario, but Thursday currently looks like the highest rain \nchances (30-60%) for areas farther west including Coconino/Yavapai\ncounties. The overall heavy rain/flood threat is low, with only \nisolated areas of heavy rain. Friday through the weekend - \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms remain in the \nforecast, though the details remain uncertain.",
